Over the past years, metal-organic frameworks (MOF) have been directly used as electrodes or as a precursor for MOF-derived materials in energy storage and conversion systems. In the wide range of existing MOF derivatives, MOF-derived layered double hydroxides (LDHs) are determined to be promising materials due to their unique structure and features. However, MOF-derived LDHs (MDL) materials can suffer from insufficient intrinsic conductivity and agglomeration during formation. Various techniques and approaches were designed and applied to tackle these problems, such as using ternary LDHs, ion-doping, sulphurization, phosphorylation, selenization, direct growth, and conductive substrates. All the mentioned enhancement techniques aim to create the ideal electrode materials with maximum performance. In this review, we gathered and discussed the most recent progressive advances, different synthesis methodologies, unsolved challenges, applications, and electro­ chemical and electrocatalytic performance of MDL materials. We hope this work will be a reliable source for future progress and synthesis of these materials.
## 1. Introduction
With more developed countries, higher energy demands, and ongoing usage of fossil fuels, we are obligated to develop better and more substitutional energy storage and conversion systems to avoid future environmental crises [1–4]. Through the numerous conducted studies on this subject, electrochemical energy storage and conversion (ESC) has gained a great deal of attention as a cutting-edge field of study [5,6]. The studied systems include supercapacitors (SCs), rechargeable batteries, water electrolyzers, fuel cells, etc. [7–13]. Supercapacitors and rechargeable batteries are the most prevalent energy storage sys­ tems made of enhanced electrodes to produce high specific capacity and endurance. Also, the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER), the oxygen evolution reaction (OER), and the oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) are the leading reactions in energy conversion systems for water splitting processes requiring efficient electrocatalysts with high catalytic activity, stability, and selectivity [14–20]. With all this in mind, finding novel, high-yield, and functional materials to meet all the favorable physico­ chemical requirements that can boost storage capacity and conversion efficiency has been challenging over the years. Existing obstacles for electrode materials include low specific surface area, insufficient redoxactive sites, poor ion transport, low electronic conductivity, inadequate selectivity, and short-term stability, which severely impede the devel­ opment of electrochemical performance and must be overcome [21].
Metal−organic frameworks (MOFs) are a brand-new class of crys­ talline porous materials with highly-ordered structures consisting of metal ions or clusters as nodes and organic ligands as linkers [22–24]. They have been extensively utilized as precursors for constructing functional materials in ESC due to their exceptional physiochemical properties, such as highly porous and organized structure, adjustable nodes and linkers, high surface area, particular morphology, and so forth [25–27]. Li et al. [28] reported multimetallic Mn-based MOF (Mn-MIL100) as a sulfur host to achieve and advanced cathode in LSB. They prepared bimetallic MnM-MIL-100 structures using different metal ions such as $\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}$ , ${\mathbb{A}}^{3+}$ , $\mathsf{C a}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ , ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ , $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ , $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ , $\mathsf{S r}^{2+}$ , $S\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ , $\mathtt{B a}^{2+}$ , $\mathsf{C e}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ , and $\mathbf{Bi}^{3+}$ , and also trimetallic MOFs like MnNiCu-MIL100 and MnNiCo-MIL-100. A facile one-step direct solvothermal reac­ tion with different reaction times and solvent volumes modified Mn
MIL-100. In order to obtain appropriate morphology and crystallinity, The optimized 3-h reaction time and $25\mathrm{\ml}$ solvent volume were determined. Among all synthesized MnM-MIL- $100@S$ composite mate­ rials, bimetallic MnNi-MIL- $100@S$ cathode revealed the best LSB per­ formance with the highest specific capacity of $1579.8\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ and an outstanding cycling performance and rate capability of 708.8 mAh ${\bf g}^{-1}$ after 200 cycles. In another work by Li and colleagues [29], they investigated hexagonal MIL-96-Al MOF with different shapes and sizes, such as platelet crystals, bipyramidal crystals, and prismatic bipyra­ midal crystals synthesized via cosolvent methods and sulfur loading process as positive electrode for batteries. It is uncovered that those with a smaller size of the bipyramidal crystals provide higher initial capacity. The highest initial specific capacities were obtained from HBC-0.78-S of $1083.4\ \mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , indicating that more sulfur can infuse into the free space of MOF and reduce the loss of active materials, nonetheless after 200 cycles, the specific capacities diminished to $448\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . Although using MOF materials in ESC are widespread, their broad application domains suffer from insufficient electronic conductivity and relatively poor chemical stability. For instance, Lions et al. studied the electro­ catalytic ORR performance of Co-Al-Porphyrin MOF (Co-Al-PMOF) [30]. Electrochemical measurements displayed an onset potential of $0.75{\mathrm{~V~}}$ versus a reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE) and a diffusion limiting current density $\mathrm{(j_{L})}$ of $-0.6\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ in $0.1\mathrm{~M~H_{2}S O_{4}}$ . In 2017, Usov et al. presented a robust PCN-223-Fe MOF by the combination of Zr6 oxo clusters and Fe(III) porphyrin linkers as ORR electrocatalysts [31]. Due to the difficulty of maintaining or enhancing the ORR kinetics of the molecular compounds in MOFs, the majority of these MOF-based materials demonstrate poor performance. Another crucial requirement for MOFs as electrocatalysts materials is high conductivity. Neverthe­ less, MOFs often insulate because to the weak conductivity of the redoxactive sites surrounded by conventional organic linkers. In addition, the weak electrical interaction between metallic nodes and organic linkers is an additional issue that affects the conductivity of MOFs [32,33]. For the issue of improving the chemical stability and poor electrical conduc­ tivity of MOFs, some strategies have been developed to overcome this dilemma, such as using MOFs as sacrificial templates and derived into various functional materials, such as metal hydroxides, oxides, sulfides, selenides, phosphides, carbon-based materials, and others. These de­ rivatives show higher chemical stability, electronic conductivity, and electrochemical performance [34–37].
Layered double hydroxides (LDHs) are a new class of layered mate­ rials with high redox activity, tunable components, and a relatively inexpensive and environmentally friendly synthesis method [38]. LDHs consist of positively charged divalent and trivalent metal hydroxide layers with water and charge-balancing anions, such as $\mathsf{N O}_{3}^{-}$ , $\mathsf{C O}_{3}^{2-}$ , $\mathrm{ClO}_{4}^{-}$ , $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ , $S O_{4}^{2-}$ , etc., dispersed in the interlayer galleries, and these compositions can be chemically modified [39–41]. Among all the investigated materials, MOF-derived LDHs (MDLs) have become an exciting topic to discuss as ESC materials. Using MOFs as a self-sacrificial template to produce LDHs would overcome the significant problems of using pure MOFs and exhibit higher chemical stability, electrical con­ ductivity, and electrochemical performance compared with pristine MOFs. It can be assumed that these features were attained from enhanced specific surface area and additional active sites due to the conversion of MOF to LDH through different template-directed strate­ gies [42–45].
Over the past years, numerous studies have been conducted, each of which investigated the influencing factors such as synthesis methods, solvent effects, metal doping, conductive substrates, phosphidation, sulfurization, and so on to achieve the ideal electrode materials in water splitting, supercapacitors, and batteries [46–52]. In this review, we discuss most of the recent papers and their achievements related to MDLs with a focus on energy storage and conversion, including super­ capacitors, water splitting, different battery types, and other applica­ tions, hoping to provide a comprehensive resource for those interested. Researchers can use this review article to compare various approaches and gain ideas for designing innovative advanced materials aimed at pushing the boundaries of the energy storage and conversion industry in the near future. Table 1 is also provided to facilitate readers in reading acronyms in the literature.
## 2. Synthesis and applications in the ESC
As explained before, template-directed strategies consist of a MOF as a template and then further modification procedures. MDLs were mainly obtained from a solvothermal MOF treatment to convert them into LDHs in an alkaline solution. However, other synthesis methods can achieve MDL materials through coprecipitation, electrodeposition, or even more straightforward strategies like stirring with or without applied temper­ ature [38,53,54]. Due to the wide range of metal sources and organic ligands, selecting MOFs has the benefits of adjustable precursors. Additionally, post-treatment procedures such as surface growth, conductive substrates, multiple metal doping, interlayer anion alter­ ation, phosphorization, sulfurization, and other modification ap­ proaches have been determined as essential factors to modify and boost the electrochemical performance of these materials, divided into two groups: first energy conversion and then energy storage systems [35,55–57].
## 2.1. Indirect synthesis strategy
For almost all the indirect approaches, in order to make a working electrode, a slurry of produced active materials, highly conductive car­ bon black, and a polymeric binder with different ratios were mixed evenly (with a higher proportion of active materials), and then, via different techniques, pasted on a conductive substrate as a current col­ lector for better performance.
## 2.1.1. Energy conversion: Water splitting
Among all the energy conversion strategies, the most appealing technique for generating hydrogen and oxygen from aqueous solutions has developed as electrochemical water splitting, working as a conve­ nient and non-polluting technique for attaining large-scale industrial hydrogen generation by using modest equipment to produce highly pure products [58–60]. Typically, two half-reactions govern this process, termed hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) and oxygen evolution reac­ tion (OER), which respectively occur at the cathode and anode [61,62]:
1) Oxidation: $2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\rightarrow\mathrm{O}_{2}+4\mathrm{H}^{+}+4\mathrm{e}^{-}\mathrm{E}^{\circ}=+1.23\mathrm{V}$ vs SHE   
2) Reduction: $4\mathrm{H}^{+}+4\mathrm{e}^{-}\rightarrow2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{E}^{\circ}=0.00\mathrm{V}$ vs SHE   
3) Overall: $2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\rightarrow2\mathrm{H}_{2}+\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{E}^{\circ}=+1.23\mathrm{V}$
There has been a rise in interest in the redox reactions between water, oxygen, and hydrogen, which are related to OER, ORR, and HER. Total water splitting is severely hampered by the considerable over­ potential and slow kinetics of both HER and OER. Due to their high activation energy, both HER and OER require significant overpotentials to operate [63–65]. As a result, sustainable, low-cost, and active bifunctional electrocatalysts are required to improve the sluggish ki­ netics of HER, OER, and ORR by minimizing overpotentials via breaking the strong chemical bonds between oxygen and hydrogen in molecular water, then diminishing the energy barriers of reactions to gain more profitable water-splitting results [66–68]. MOF-derived materials have the privileges of morphology and porosity, exposed redox-active sites, facile mass/charge transport, well-dispersed metal species in precursors, and particular functional groups in ORR/OER/HER electrocatalysis [69,70]. Based on the above, many different approaches should be explored to achieve the best materials as electrocatalysts.
As electrocatalysts, NiFe-LDHs are good examples of non-precious materials derived from MOFs. For example, an ultra-thin NiFe LDH derived from Ni-BDC MOF was reported by Dong et al. [71] through a surface conversion method to address the low accessibility of active sites in OER. The obtained Ni-BDC@NiFe-LDH-2 exhibited a low over­ potential of $272\mathrm{mV}$ at a current density of $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , a slight Tafel slope of $45\mathrm{~mV~dec}^{-1}$ , and by using chronoamperometry (CP) at a cur­ rent density of $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , the stability of Ni-BDC@NiFe-LDH was measured. The potential value has only lost $5.7\%$ (overpotential upward trend) within $30\mathrm{h}$ , showing outstanding stability. A bimetallic MOF was used by Rinawati et al. [72]. The NiFe-MOF-74 was subjected to a hy­ drothermal process in an alkaline solution until the formation of NiFe LDH. Then the electroactive materials with polymer binders were pasted on Ni foam (NF) without further treatments. The porous configuration inherited from the precursor provides active sites and high contact areas for electrocatalytic OER. The NiFe LDH displayed higher electroactivity with a low overpotential of $299\mathrm{mV}$ (at a current density of $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ and a lower Tafel slope of $48.7\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{dec}^{-1}$ . The durability was tested via the CP in $1.0\mathrm{M}$ KOH at a current density of $10\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and showed outstanding stability over $^{24\mathrm{~h~}}$ with a somewhat increased potential trend $(\sim5\%$ loss) that might be caused by the partial conversion of MOFderived LDH into $\mathrm{NiOO^{-}/F e O O^{-}}$ . Also, Yadav et al. [73] utilized FeMIL-88 MOF as the template and, in a facile way, combined it with nickel acetate tetrahydrate under reflux conditions to prepare NiFe-LDH that later exhibited higher catalytic activity (continual operating po­ tential at nearly $1.61\mathrm{V}$ for $2000s_{\mathrm{.}}$ , overpotential of $0.38{\mathrm{V}}$ , and electrode stability of over two hours) than the commercial OER catalyst, platinum graphitized carbon $\mathrm{(Pt/C)}$ . The durability of NiFe-LDH was also exam­ ined. At a potential of $2.28{\mathrm{~V~}}$ vs. RHE (OER conditions) in 3600 s, the time dependence of current density for NiFe-LDH and $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ was ob­ tained. A $5.3\%$ decrease in current density revealed its exceptional stability compared to Pt/C $(\sim10\%)$ .
Other approaches to increasing the electrocatalytic performance of materials include doping some elements such as phosphor, sulfur, or rare-earth elements into the structures. For instance, He et al. [74] re­ ported a novel post-treatment strategy to achieve enhanced electrodes for OER, Fig. 1a. They first derived NiCo-LDH from surfactant-mediate as-synthesized ZIF-67 at ambient temperature, then the organic li­ gands were altered to amorphous carbon through the phosphidation process with $\mathrm{NaH_{2}P O_{2}}$ . The as-modified NiCoP/C nanoboxes retained the cubic morphology and the structure size of the MOF precursor and presented excellent electrocatalytic activity with a low overpotential of $330\mathrm{mV}$ at a current density of $10\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ as well as long-term stability. The stability was investigated at $1.58{\mathrm{V}} $ , representing $96.5\%$ of the initial current after $^{10\mathrm{~h~}}$ for the NiCoP/C nanoboxes, which was much higher than the current losses of NiCoP nanoboxes $(13\%)$ and NiCo-LDH nanoboxes $(40\%)$ .
Additionally, Xu et al. [75] published single-step microencapsulation using cerium as a rare-earth element. In this paper, they used an inno­ vative synthesis method to derive Ni-Fe-Ce-LDH on the outer layer of MIL-88A as a precursor in the presence of Ni and Ce salts in a one-step solvothermal reaction. Doping $\mathsf{C e}^{3+}$ ions into the structure can cause more steadiness after the formation of MIL-88A, particularly in weak alkaline environments. This directly affects the improvement of the electronic properties (with an overpotential of $242~\mathrm{mV}$ at $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and lasting durability for $24\mathrm{h}$ ) due to cerium multivalence and adaptive coordination properties along with strong attraction for hard oxygen donors. The operative stability of $30\%$ dopant Ce was investigated by long-term CP before and after 500 cycles for at least $24\mathrm{h}$ , demonstrating insignificant alteration after 500 cycles. As a result, by adapting the amount of urea in the solution and Ce ions, the synthesis of Ni-Fe-CeLDH will be a controllable method to obtain optimized morphology and crystallographic stability other properties.
As a matter of the effect of experimental parameters, Wang and colleagues also synthesized flower-like LDH nanosheet aggregates (CoLDH FNSAs) after the sonication of Co nitrate and 2-methylimidazole to evaluate the influence of experimental variables [76]. The results of their studies are listed in the following: a) due to the weak alkalinity of 2-MIM, the hydrolysis of Co(II) ions will be interrupted and no Co-LDH FNSAs will be produced; b) the size and morphology are unaffected by the concentration of 2-MIM; c) the reaction solution, which can be methanol or methanol-water, has an important influence on the final shape of Co-LDH FNSAs, as an example, only hexagonal ${\mathrm{Co}}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ nanoplates are obtained in pure water with no methanol in the media; and d) because of the significant effect of ultrasonication in this process, increasing the reaction time and temperature to $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ and $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ , respec­ tively, produces a minimal amount of Co-LDH FNSAs. After ${10,000s}_{\mathrm{.}}$ , CoLDH FNSAs still retains $58\%$ of its primary OER current value while $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ nanoparticles only upholds $35\%$ of its initial value. In addition, the admirable stability, a low overpotential of $300\mathrm{mV}$ at $10\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ , and a slight Tafel slope of $110\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{dec}^{-1}$ were obtained by OER in an alkaline environment.
Recently, more complicated methods have been used to construct new advanced materials. For instance, Huang et al. synthesized MIL$101@\mathrm{OX-MOF}$ and, in the next step, transformed them to MIL$\boldsymbol{101@}$ NiFe-LDH, employing a novel “MOF on MOF” method as shown in Fig. 1f [77]. The as-obtained material exhibited an overpotential of $215\mathrm{mV}$ at $10\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ and a small Tafel slope of $55.1\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{{\bar{dec}}^{-1}}$ in the oxygen evolution reaction related to the high phase interfaces of the derivative composites inherited from the mother structure. MIL101@NiFe-LDH showed great stability outcome during $24\mathrm{~h~}$ stability test at 1.47 V vs. RHE and it reveals that its OER activity remains the same after 3000 cycles.
In another study reported by Chen and colleagues, the conversion of ZIF-9 (I) into ZIF-9 (III), ZIF-9 (III)/Co-LDH, and Co-LDH was investi­ gated through a controllable chemical reaction [78]. In this method, the solvent composition ratio greatly influences the final structure of the products. In the presence of deionized water as the only component in the solvent, only 2D ZIF-9 (III) was produced. However, the combination of ethanol and water in the solution can result in the formation of LDH, indicating the ratio of water/ethanol as a crucial factor in the phase conversion (Fig. 1g). The as-derived ZIF-9 (III)/Co-LDH in a solvent made up of 15:30 parts water to ethanol optimized OER performance with a low overpotential of $297~\mathrm{mV}$ at $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , a low Tafel slope $(65~\mathrm{mV}~\mathrm{dec}^{-1})$ . The long-term durability was tested after continuous testing for $10\mathrm{{h}}$ by CP measurements at a constant current density of 10 mA $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , exhibiting the $\mathfrak{n}$ value of ZIF-9 (III)/Co-LDH-15 remained steady with the minimum degradation, hence demonstrating better stability. In this case, not only various metal sites and large specific surface area but also the solvent ratio and interactions between ZIF-9 (III) and Co-LDH are responsible for the great OER results.
## 2.1.2. Energy storage: Supercapacitors
In recent years, supercapacitors (SCs) have attracted considerable interest in energy storage due to their unique features, such as safe and rapid charging and discharging and higher power densities. However, their comparatively low energy density prevents them from being widely utilized [5,79]. SCs can perform as electrical double-layer capacitive (EDLC), pseudocapacitive charge storage, or a mix of both through non-faradaic and partially faradaic processes, in which they present surface-controlled and diffusion-controlled kinetics, respec­ tively [80]. One of the most effective techniques to enhance the energy density of the EDLC and pseudocapacitance mechanisms is first to un­ derstand their limits of efficiency and then to chemically design and synthesize optimal porous electrode materials with rich redox sites that make them more electrically conductive [81,82]. In this regard, MDLs have been characterized as promising materials owing to their various morphologies, composing elements, and synthesis methods. However, different MOFs are used, but ZIF-67 and other ZIF derivatives are still the most favorable MOFs as sacrificial templates [83,84].
In the most recent study by Zhang et al. [85] in 2022, they used ZIF67 as the sacrificial template to derive NiCo-LDH through two different methods: via room temperature and hydrothermal processes, resulting in hollow nanocage and sea urchin morphologies, respectively. The morphology of the materials has been discovered to have a significant impact on the final electrochemical performance. Electrochemical ki­ netic research showed that hollow NiCo-LDH has a considerably higher capacitive charge storage capability than the other, demonstrating that a quick surface redox reaction occurs throughout the electrochemical process, resulting in better electrochemical properties. Also, its assem­ bled asymmetric supercapacitor (ASC) exhibited a high energy density of 88.6 Wh $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ at $749.9\mathrm{W}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ .
Using additives also has a dynamic role in the final shape, size con­ sistency, and progressive growth of LDHs on the surface of the sacrificial template. Usman Tahir et al. [86] have investigated the impact of the existence of binary additives on the morphology and electrochemical performance. They added binary bridging ligands, such as 2,3,5,6-tera­ fluroterephthalic acid (H2tfbdc), hexamethylenetetramine (HMT), and nickel nitrate in the etching process and synthesized different LDHs with various modified factors such as reaction temperature and time, as well as with or without additives. Among these synthesized materials, (NiCo$\mathrm{\DeltaLDH)_{SHH}}$ with both $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ tfbdc and HMT induced in its structure had the neatest, well-defined hexagonal micro-flower structure that revealed the best supercapacitor performance attributable to the enriched redox metal centers and its large surface area (Fig. 2a). At a current density of 1 A $\displaystyle{\boldsymbol{\mathrm{g}}}^{-1}$ , the highest specific capacity was $1765\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ , with consistent cycling behavior (sustained $86\%$ of specific capacitance after $5000\mathrm{GCD}$ cycles). Additionally, after 6000 cycles in an ASC device with activated carbon (AC) a high energy density of $53.31\mathrm{Wh}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ and a power density of 749.7 W $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ with $84.3\%$ capacitance retention were achieved. Shang et al. [87] studied the transformation of ZIF-67 into hollow porous NiCoLDH at different periods of the aging process and the effect of residual pristine MOF in the final materials on the electrode performance ac­ cording to Fig. 2 (b-f). The ZIF-67 nanoparticles (NPs) may not transform completely into LDH during etching, reducing ion transport in the electrode and electrolyte. Also, the ZIF residuals affected electrolyte ion diffusion efficiency and faradaic resistance between the electrolyte/ electrode interfaces. Furthermore, while a more extended treatment can successfully eliminate ZIF-67 residuals and higher crystallinity for NiCoLDH, over-crystallization would cause lower electrochemical active sites. It was found that the sufficient time to attain optimized NiCo-LDH with high crystallinity is $4.5\mathrm{~h~}$ . This optimized electrode displayed an unprecedented charge storage capacity of $732\mathrm{C}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at a current density of $1\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}$ and kept its capacitance up to $68.3\%$ . Also, the ASC system presented an excellent energy density of 45.33 Wh $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ at a power density of $395.13~\mathrm{W~kg}^{-1}$ with durability of $78.55\%$ of the original capacitance after 8000 cycles. Yu and colleagues [88] employed un­ solvable crystals of zinc nitrate hydroxide as both a $Z\mathrm{n}$ source and a sacrificial template to obtain the same morphology for ZIF-8 nanoflake arrays (ZIF-NFA). In the next step, ZIF-8 NFA in the presence of nickel nitrate or cobalt nitrate in methanol played as the secondary sacrificial structure and turned into 2D hollow LDH nanoparticles. Consequently, the ASC cell of LDH containing Ni or Co demonstrated an excellent ca­ pacity and a high specific energy density of 52.1 Wh $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ at a specific power density of $845~\mathrm{W~kg^{-1}}$ . The device could still sustain an energy density of $39.3\mathrm{Wh}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ even at a high-power density of $16.5\mathrm{kW}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ In a novel study conducted by Ramachandran et al. [89], a Ni-BTC MOF was used instead of Co-based MOFs and then transformed into NiCe-LDH at various synthesis periods. Interestingly, in addition to the flower-like microsphere structure of NiCe-LDH, rod-like structures are observed in the SEM images, attributed to the combination of Ce nitrate salt and organic linkers of Ni-MOF and the formation of rod-like Ce-MOF. By increasing the etching time up to 10 and $24\mathrm{h}$ , the conversion progressed and sharper NiCe-LDHs crystals with smaller Ce-MOF nanorods were gained. The optimal NiCe-LDH was formed after a $^{10\mathrm{~h~}}$ process with well-supplied redox areas, exhibiting an extreme specific capacitance of $1500.6\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ and a high energy density of 13.86 Wh $\mathbf{kg}^{-\bar{1}}$ with great cyclic stability in an ASC device.
Additionally, some studies involved bimetallic MOFs as sacrificial templates to enhance the conductivity of materials. For example, in another paper, Usman Tahir et al. [90] synthesized CoNi-ZIF as the template and then converted it into 2D CoNi-LDH via two hierarchical, facile, and inexpensive steps at room temperature and in an aqueous solution without using any surfactants. In this water-assisted co-pre­ cipitation approach, to produce CoNi-LDH, different mass ratios of CoNiZIF to excessive $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ were used, and 1:4 appeared to be the appropriate ratio, named CoNi-LDH4. The adjusted CoNi-LDH4 had a specific capacitance of $1877\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at a current density of 1 A ${\bf g}^{-1}$ and a long cycle life of $99.89\%$ after 5000 cycles. The ASC device exhibited a consistent cyclability of $92.13\%$ after 5000 cycles and an energy density of 49.3 Wh $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ at $750\mathrm{W}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ output voltage. Cao and co-workers [91] have also converted CoNi-BDC MOF into a sheet-like NiCo-LDH with superb electrochemical performance. The sheet-like structure provides a better contact area and more routes for the diffusion of the electrolyte through the active materials for faradic redox reactions. The measure­ ments revealed a specific capacitance of $1265\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at a current density of 1 A ${\bf g}^{-1}$ , which is greater than CoNi-MOF $(570\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ at $1\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1})$ , as well as high cyclic constancy with a comparatively low resistance of $84\%$ retention after 1000 cycles. Zheng et al. [92] used a binary CoMnZIF precursor to form a ternary NiCoMn-LDH through a refluxing pro­ cess, and added $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ to the mixture of CoMn-ZIF and ethanol. The resultant ternary LDH significantly surpassed the electrochemical performance of the binary NiCo-LDH, exposing a high specific capacity of $2012.5\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ at $1\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}$ and an excellent capacity rate of $75.0\%$
retention at $10\mathrm{A}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ .
Recently, many studies have focused on the design and synthesis of complicated and advanced materials to acquire more enhanced features, resulting in more conductivity and capacity. Using transition metal sulfides (TMSs) is an effective manner to boost the electrochemical performance of materials. For instance, as illustrated in Fig. 2g, in an innovative approach, Wang et al. [93] used $\mathbf{MnO}_{2}$ nanotubes (NT) as a skeleton to form ZIF-67 after electrostatic absorption and nucleation of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ on the surface. In the next stage, the as-prepared $\mathrm{MnO}_{2}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}67$ transformed into $\mathrm{MnO_{2}@N i C o-L D H}$ hollow nanocages via a hydrolysis procedure. The $\mathrm{MnO}_{2}@\mathrm{NiCo-LDH}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}$ was subsequently produced with secondary heat treatment and the addition of thioacetamide (TAA). As expected, the placement of this core-shell structure on the $\mathbf{MnO}_{2}$ NTs thoroughly prevents the aggregation of active materials. The morpho­ logical characterizations are detailed in Fig. 2 (h-o). Also, the existence of nanocage LDHs along with the cobalt sulfides enhances the electro­ chemical properties. As a result, the electrode materials have shown a high specific capacitance of $1547\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ and $1189\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ at current densities of $1\mathrm{A}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $10\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ , respectively. The high-rate efficiency of $76.9\%$ , high durability of $82.3\%$ after 2000 cycles, the maximum energy density of $50\mathrm{~Wh~kg^{-1}}$ , and a maximum power density of 9658 W ${\bf k g}^{-1}$ in an ASC setup confirm the superior performance of $\mathrm{MnO}_{2}@\mathrm{NiCo}-$ $\mathrm{LDH/CoS_{2}}$ nanocages. In another paper, Li et al. [94] used a multi-step conversion method to convert ZIF-67 into $\mathrm{C-NiCo-LDH/Co_{9}S_{8}}$ hollow nanocages, Fig. 2p. In this recent procedure, the as-synthesized ZIF-67 underwent an annealing process in an ${\bf N}_{2}$ atmosphere at $400^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ resulting in an increment of amorphous carbon in the structure. Then the enriched carbon structure, ZIF-67-C, was employed as a template to grow $\mathsf{C o g S8}$ nanoparticles after adding of thioacetamide to the mixture. Later, by depositing of metallic sulfur NPs on the ZIF-67-C, NiCo-LDH was formed on the surface of $\mathsf{C o g S8}$ via a hydrothermal process. The SEM images of all mentioned materials are provided in Fig. 2 (q-v). Intro­ ducing metal sulfides to the structure benefits the transfer of electrons through the layers, the lower electronegativity of sulfur than oxygen, and the more diverse nanostructures when sulfur is replaced with oxy­ gen, resulting in better electrical conductivity. The $\mathrm{C-LDH/Co_{9}S_{8}}$ hollow structure demonstrated outstanding specific capacitance of $1654\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ at a current density of 2 A ${\bf g}^{-1}$ and $82.5\%$ capacity maintenance after 5000 cycles. Moreover, a high energy density of $47.3\mathrm{Wh}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ at a high power density of $1505\mathrm{W}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ appeared in a prepared ASC device. Wang et al. [95] reported a unique structure consisting of encapsulated ZIF-67 by exfoliated MXene, $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}},$ and then consequential solvothermal conversion into a 3D flower-like $\mathrm{NiCo-LDH/Ti_{3}C_{2}T_{x}},$ where homoge­ neous MXene nanosheets (NS), as a conductive network, strongly interact with NiCo-LDH, enhancing the electrochemical performance (Fig. 3a). The as-prepared $\mathrm{NiCo-LDH/Ti_{3}C_{2}T_{x}}{\cdot}5$ (synthesized with $5\mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathrm{Ti_{3}C_{2}T_{x})}$ exposed a high specific capacity of $635.7\mathrm{C}\ g^{-1}$ at $1\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}$ and endured $382.3\mathrm{C}\ g^{-1}$ at $20\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}$ . The related ASC achieved a high energy density of $44.6\mathrm{W}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ at a power density of $852.5\mathrm{W}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ with an outstanding cycling capacitance of $85.71\%$ retention after 10,000 cycles. Lee and colleagues [96] designed an advanced Hollow-In-Hollow (HIH) structure through hierarchical solvothermal procedures. As depicted in the Fig. 3b, in the first step, the newly synthesized $\mathrm{{SiO}_{2}}$ microspheres and carbon source dopamine hydrochloride (DA) were mixed and produced polydopamine-coated $\mathrm{SiO_{2}}$ microspheres $(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}@\mathrm{PDA})$ via polymerization. By further heat treatment and carbonization under ${\mathrm{N}}_{2},$ , the $\mathrm{{siO}_{2}}$ hard cores were finally dissolved and removed, resulting in the acquired hollow carbon spheres (HCSs). After a secondary growth of ZIF-67 on HCS, the sacrificial template was ob­ tained, $\operatorname{HCS}\textcircled{a}\operatorname{ZIF-}67$ . In the last step, the MOF template on HCS trans­ formed into hollow NiCo-LDHs (HIH-LDH), increasing the ion transfer through the prepared double hollow system. HIH-LDHs delivered a specific capacity of $156.4\:\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{h}\:\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at ${\mathsf{5}}{\mathrm{A}}{\mathsf{g}}^{-1}$ and a long cycle life with $73.4\%$ stability of capacity after 10,000 cycles, revealing the enhanced structural characteristics and capacity as a supercapacitor. Similarly, a mesoporous plasma-reduced graphene oxide (MPRGO) as the negative material was assembled in a hybrid supercapacitor and displayed a maximum energy density of $34.5~\mathrm{Wh}~\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ and a high power density of $55\mathrm{kW}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ . In 2020, Liu et al. [97] utilized an LDH derived from ZIF-67 as a secondary template for further transformation to achieve better supercapacitive materials. In this work, ZIF-67 played a role as a pri­ mary self-sacrificial template to assemble nanocube NiCo-LDHs through a co-precipitation process. Next, NiCo-LDH itself is used as a secondary sacrificial template to form $(\mathrm{Ni},\mathrm{Co})\mathsf{S e}_{2}$ through an anion-exchange process after the addition of NaHSe in a solvothermal reaction. In the final step, the prepared $(\mathrm{Ni},\mathrm{Co})\mathsf{S e}_{2}$ nanocubes were integrated with reduced graphene oxide (rGO), developing $(\mathrm{Ni},\mathrm{Co}){\sf S e}_{2}@\mathrm{rG}0$ under the ultrasonication process. Fig. 3c demonstrates that the presence of Ni and Co transition metals, along with the low electronegative Se element, are advantageous for altering the electrical structure. Furthermore, the presence of porous rGO in the hybrid structure benefits conductivity (as a conductive network), mechanical flexibility (as a space separator), and structural integrity (as a structure protector). Likewise, in all the pre­ vious papers, the as-prepared active materials were coated on the proper current collector (in this case, nickel foam) and presented a high specific capacity of $649.1\mathrm{C}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $1\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ , extremely high-rate performance with a superior $90.5\%$ retention of charge/discharge stability after 5000 cy­ cles at $10\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}$ . Moreover, the ASC of $(\mathrm{Ni},\mathrm{Co})\mathsf{S e}_{2}@\mathrm{r}\mathsf{G O}$ and AC indi­ cated a high energy density of $52.6\mathrm{Wh}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ at $803.4\mathrm{W}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ and almost $100\%$ cycling retention over 10,000 cycles. In more practical applica­ tions, the $(\mathrm{Ni},\mathrm{Co}){\bf S}\mathrm{e}_{2}@\mathrm{rGO}//\mathrm{AC}$ illuminated red, green, and blue LED bulbs for a minimum of five minutes.
## 2.2. Direct synthesis strategy
The most common form of nearly all the MDLs is powder. The powder form needs to attach to the surface of the electrode for further examination in water splitting and supercapacitor. Therefore, adding polymeric binders to stabilize the active materials on the surface of the working electrode is essential [98]. However, spreading the active ma­ terials causes agglomeration of nanoparticles, binding agents reduce active reaction sites and ion transport and increase the interfacial resistance, and peeling off the attached materials during the stability test, are the significant dilemmas of this electrode preparation method [36,99]. To overcome such concerns, using binder-free electrodes through the direct growth of materials on a conductive substrate such as carbon-based materials, nickel foam, or other substrates not only am­ plifies the electrical conductivity of MDL but also increases the number of active sites [100,101].
## 2.2.1. Energy conversion: Water splitting
Wang et al. [102] synthesized NiFe-BDC MOF via in situ formations of MOF particles on NF (Fig. 4a). This paper emphasized the electro­ catalytic effects of edge or corner sites through edge-enriched (EE) NiFeLDH nanosheet arrays through either the process of exfoliating LDHs to a thickness equal to the unit cell or through the reduction of their size to extremely small NPs. Several characterizations and computational cal­ culations confirmed that these edges accommodate abundant iron and oxygen vacant positions, improving the electrical state of NiFe-LDH as active sites, consequently causing enhanced electrocatalytic OER ac­ tivity, with an overpotential of $205~\mathrm{mV}$ at a current density of $10~\mathrm{{mA}}$ $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . The operational durability of the EE-NiFe-LDH array at $100~\mathrm{{mA}}$ $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ was evaluated for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ and presented $95\%$ retention of current density.
Zhou and others reported a porous 2D NiCoSe@NF as a superior electrocatalyst in OER and HER [103]. In this three-step hydrothermal process as shown in Fig. 4b, they first synthesized ZIF-67 on nickel foam and then, through an ion exchange process, transferred it into NiCo$\mathrm{LDH@NF_{:}}$ an improvement in its wettability and better water contacts due to the hydrophilic characteristics of derived LDH. Afterward, they modified the final structure via further selenization via the reaction of $\mathsf{s e}^{2-}$ ion with Ni and Co ions in the LDH to produce NiCoSe@NF nanosheets. It is worth mentioning that in the presence of 2 mmol of selenium powder, not only sheet-like LDH nanoparticles but also NiSe nanowires were directly obtained on the NF, providing additional active sites and faster electron transfer to upsurge the OER activity. And presence of 8 mmol of selenium results in HER activity. To achieve the current densities of $10\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ and $20\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , respectively, an overpotential of $170~\mathrm{mV}$ for HER and $278~\mathrm{mV}$ for OER was required. Moreover, the HER stability test of the NiCoSe nanosheets was observed at the current density of $10\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ , and the potential was preserved for over $40\mathrm{h}$ without noticeable change. Similarly, for OER stability, the sample also displayed a slight decay after $40\mathrm{h}$ . Also, the electrocatalysts yield a stable current density of 10 and $100\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ for $48\mathrm{h}$ , approving the long-term stability. The NiCoSe@NF was used as the anode and cathode in an overall water splitting, producing a current density of 10 $\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ at a low voltage of $1.51\mathrm{V}$ and long-term durability as a result of its excellent bifunctional catalytic activity.
In another study, a “MOF-on-MOF” strategy was used by Zhang et al. [104] to obtain an enhanced material for OER. Firstly, due to the remarkable performance of Ni-MOFs in OER, they synthesized a Ni-MOF using terephthalic acid and nickel chloride salt as precursors on NF. The excessive carboxylic acid groups of Ni-MOF are potential sites for oxa­ late MOF (Ox-MOF) growth after adding potassium trioxalatoferrate (III) trihydrate to the mixture. In alkaline solutions, the $_{0\mathbf{x}}$ -MOF con­ verts into NiFe-LDH without any morphological deformation. The “MOF-on-MOF” growth and further conversion into Ni-MOF/LDH on NF provide a high specific surface area, numerous reaction sites, and a wellprepared phase interface for electron transfer, demonstrating the superb electrocatalytic performance of OER that can be seen in Fig. 4 (c-l). For the oxidation of water, merely a minor overpotential of $220~\mathrm{mV}$ at 10 mA $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ was required for Ni-MOF/LDH, which is insignificant compared to the required overpotentials for LDH, Ni-MOF, and NF electrodes (Fig. $4\mathrm{m})$ . The stability test of Ni-MOF/LDH electrode was conducted via CP, and after $20\mathrm{h}$ of operating, the relative potential rose by $5.8\%$ . Also, no considerable change was detected in morphological characterization. Moreover, the $Z\mathrm{n}$ -air battery stability performance was conducted at $10\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ for 600 cycles and the battery efficiency diminished to $57.9\%$ in the last cycle, indicating its excellent stability.
One optimizing method for MDLs is using MXenes as a matrix in the structure. Hu and colleagues [105] first covered NF with $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ MXene by plunging it into the MXene solution, forming a thin homogenous nanosheet layer of $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ MXene on the surface that was used as a sup­ porting matrix for in situ formations of ZIF-67 by taking advantage of numerous functional groups such as-O and-OH on the surface. After adding iron salt in an alkaline environment, as-produced ZIF-67 was progressively etched and coprecipitated with Fe ions and certain anions to obtain CoFe LDH with rich active sites. Because of the strong elec­ tronic coupling and inevitable interfacial bonding between CoFe-LDH and $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ MXene, the generated $\mathrm{CoFe-LDH/Ti_{3}C_{2}}$ demonstrates notable experimental and theoretical OER performance with extremely low overpotentials of just 170 and $238\mathrm{mV}$ to reach the current densities of 10 and $100\mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , respectively, in Fig. 4 (n). This outcome could massively boost the inherent electrocatalyst activity of the surface and successfully enhance the OER intermediates. Throughout the multistep CP study, the current density was increased from 20 to $180\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-\bar{2}}$ , and no noticeable degradation is seen throughout each step, indicating that CoFe-LDH/Ti3C2 has excellent conductivity and rapid mass trans­ port over OER. In addition, the long-term durability of CoFe-LDH/Ti3C2 at $j=50\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ was investigated. After approximately $50\mathrm{h}$ of testing, the current density remained unchanged, indicating that the OER sta­ bility of $\mathrm{CoFe-LDH/Ti_{3}C_{2}}$ is remarkable.
Ternary metal LDHs are promising new approaches in super­ capacitors and overall water-splitting applications. Qin et al. [106] synthesized ternary metal sulfides as an efficient and highly active OER electrode. They utilized NF as a supporting substrate, and through further nuclearization and the formation of ZIF-67 on the surface, CoMOF/NF was obtained. In the next step, by soaking Co-MOF/NF in $\mathrm{Na_{2}M o O_{4}}$ solution and further ion exchange process, the MoCo-LDH was achieved. Next, by adding Ni salt and further sulfurization of LDH with thioacetamide as a sulfur source via a hydrothermal method, MoCoNiS on NF was obtained. Electrochemical examinations show that MoCoNiS/ NF is more active for water oxidation than mono-or bimetallic sulfides. Also, inducing Mo ions into the structure might control the electronic conditions near Co and Ni, indicating the possibility of synergistic in­ teractions between various heteroatoms. This nanostructure is distinc­ tive among other electrocatalyst materials that have been previously reported since it only requires 151 and $226\mathrm{mV}$ overpotential to achieve current densities of 10 and $100\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ , respectively. The synergistic interactions between Mo, Co, and Ni metals, the decrement of the energy barriers in the ternary metal structure, the acceptable usage of active sites, the effortless releasing of $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ gas, and the use of a 3D NF as a conductive substrate are the main reasons attributed to the outstanding OER performance of MoCoNiS/NF. Moreover, the electrocatalyst exhibited stable OER activity for over $10\mathrm{{h}}$ with no considerable current drop, confirming its high stability.
In addition to Ni foam as a highly conductive and synergetic substrate, other metal foams are also available as supporting substrates. As illustrated in Fig. 4 (o), Yin et al. [107] utilized Cu foam (CuF) and formed $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ nanowires directly on the surface of CuF through sur­ face chemical oxidation, providing additional active sites. Afterward, they fabricated defect-rich porous nanocrystals of bimetallic ZIF (CoZnZIF) on nanowires at room temperature. Finally, by a facile hydrother­ mal process, the $\mathrm{Cu(OH)}_{2}$ and CoZn-ZIF in the presence of cobalt-and zinc nitrate salts in an alcohol environment transform into CuO and CoZn-LDH, respectively. The final product, CuO@CoZn-LDH/CuF, exhibited a well-organized morphology (Fig. 4p-r), high surface area, and low contact resistance, boosting the electrocatalytic efficiency in water splitting. The as-produced CuO@CoZn-LDH/CuF reveals an outstanding performance in alkaline conditions with low overpotentials of $194\mathrm{mV}$ for OER and $124~\mathrm{mV}$ for HER at the same current density of $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ . In consideration of commercialization, it is necessary to evaluate the stability of the OER electrode. After $40\mathrm{~h~}$ in alkaline solu­ tion, the $\mathrm{CuO@CoZn-LDH/CuF}$ electrode demonstrated great durability with a stable overpotential, indicating potential electrode in catalytic activities for long-term operation and commercial use on a wide scale. Also, as a bifunctional electrode for complete water splitting, it plays a role as an alkali-electrolyzer with a low cell voltage of $1.55\mathrm{V}$ at a current density of $10\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ for more than $48\mathrm{h}$ , showing superior activity and similar to the $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}@\mathrm{CF}|$ $|\mathrm{Pt/C}@\mathrm{CF}$ commercial couple catalyst.
Ye and colleagues directly developed MDL materials on the surface of iron foam (IF) as a supporting and conductive substrate with high electrical conductivity and surface area [108]. This substrate also pro­ vides the iron source for further synthesis of Fe-MOF on IF. After the fabrication of Fe-MOF, through adding Ni to the media and a hydro­ thermal process, the surface of the MOF is partially transformed to NiFeLDH, Fig. 5a. By changing the amount of $\mathrm{NiCl}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ and reaction time to $_{0.1\ g}$ and $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ , respectively, as illustrated in Fig. 5(b-e), the optimized flower-like NiFe-LDH@Fe-MOF/IF-2 was achieved, showing admirable OER activity in alkaline electrolyte arising from the in situ hierarchical structure growth, the strong synergistic effect between induced-Ni and Fe in LDH and MOF, and the highly conductive IF. The CP curves demonstrated the high durability of NiFe-LDH@Fe-MOF/IF-2 after $20\mathrm{h}$ of stability testing at $10~\mathrm{\mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . NiFe-LDH $@$ Fe-MOF/IF-2 only required overpotentials of 183, 237, and $257\mathrm{mV}$ to reach current den­ sities of 10, 100, and $200\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ , respectively, and also a minimum of $20\mathrm{h}$ durability (Fig. 5f-k).
Additionally, various carbon-based materials were used as substrates in binder-free electrodes due to their rich active sites and conductivity. For instance, carbon fiber cloth (CFC) was used in a paper reported by Chen et al. as the substrate and experienced further modifications [109]. As shown in Fig. 5 (l-m), their unique approach produced a bifunctional electrocatalyst for HER and OER. They first synthesized CoZn-ZIF on the CFC, then NiCoZn-LDH was obtained using facile ion exchange and etching processes along with the addition of Ni nitrate. Then, the ob­ tained LDH was subjected to phosphorization followed by an annealing process to obtain NiCoZnP nanoparticles on N-doped carbon (NC) nanosheets, benefiting from nano-sized NiCoZnP particles with rich reactive sites and highly conductive NCs that provide fast charge transfer pathways. Consequently, the NiCoZnP/NC arrays demonstrate a low overpotential of $74\mathrm{mV}$ and an overpotential of $228\mathrm{mV}$ for HER and OER activity to achieve a current density of $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , as well as unique overall water splitting with a low potential of $1.54\mathrm{V}$ to reach 10 $\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , and also display excellent stability with slight current density loss during $45\mathrm{~h~}$ of testing at a cell voltage of $1.54\mathrm{V}$ , which is signifi­ cantly superior to that of $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ cathode electrode and $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}$ anode under $1.65\mathrm{V}$ .
Wang and co-workers [110] reported an outstanding performance as an electrode for HER using non-noble-metals on carbon cloth (CC). This paper used a well-designed hierarchical synthesis procedure to achieve an advanced material with notable activity. Firstly, leaf-like Co-ZIF MOFs (ZIF-L-Co) were directly grown on CC at room temperature by a typical procedure of ZIF synthesis. Second, Co-MOF/CC was annealed under an Ar atmosphere at $650~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to form Co NPs encapsulated in a porous Co-N-doped carbon (CNC) framework on CC. Finally, $\mathsf{C o@C N C}$ nanoarrays/CC underwent further annealing at $400^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under the same Ar atmosphere and additional Se vapor to achieve embedded $\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}\mathrm{NPs}$ within CNC layers on CC, Fig. 5n. The $\mathsf{C S@C N C}$ NAs/CC electrocatalyst demonstrated excellent HER performance and a long lifespan with almost zero deterioration after $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ . It also showed a comparatively small Tafel slope of $38\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{dec}^{-1}$ , and an extremely low overpotential of $84~\mathrm{mV}$ at $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , surpassing the majority of transition metal sel­ enides (TMSe) and other progressive materials. The structural con­ stancy, numerous catalytic active sites, easy electrolyte access, facile diffusion of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ bubbles, well-spread $\mathsf{C o S e}_{2}$ NPs, highly porous and conductive CNC matrix, and the synergetic effect between them play vital roles in enhancing the HER performance of the as-produced elec­ trode. Stability in the HER process is an important feature for a viable electrocatalyst. In order to determine the HER stability of $\mathrm{CS}@\mathrm{CNCNA}s/$ CC, a 2000-cycle CV test was conducted in the voltage range of $-0.346\mathrm{V}$ to $+0.254\mathrm{~V~}$ . (vs RHE). Using a chronoamperometry (CA) measure­ ments, the stability of $\mathsf{C S@C N C}$ NAs under a continuous overpotential of $84~\mathrm{mV}$ (vs RHE) for $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ was examined deeper. After a long-term HER procedure, the current density of $10\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ slightly degraded, con­ firming the exceptional stability.
## 2.2.2. Energy storage: Supercapacitors
As said before, using flexible carbon materials such as carbon fibers (CF) and carbon cloth as substrates is common in ESC due to their conductivity and numerous electroactive sites. Zhu and colleagues [111] reported an efficient approach to synthesizing a high-performance supercapacitor. They used transition-metal selenization and iondoping techniques. First, through a hydrothermal process, CoZn-ZIF MOFs were grown on oxidized flexible CC as a supporting substrate, and in the next step, in the Se atmosphere, porous CoZn-ZIF endured a selenization process, and MOF-Se was obtained. The MOF-Se arrays work as a bridging agent between the conductive CC and active syn­ thesized materials that can lessen the contact resistance and enhance charge transport in the system. Also, selenization treatment directly affected the morphology by creating a rougher structure and more curvy edges. Lastly, through an electrodeposition method and by adding extra Co and Ni salts to the solution and simultaneous doping of $\mathbf{Mo^{6+}}$ , Modoped LDHs@MOF-Se was achieved. The ion-doping process is also beneficial for increasing the electrochemical performance and pro­ longing the cycle stability. In particular, $\mathbf{Mo}^{6+}$ cations with minor ion radius and high positive valence generate ultra-high redox positions in the structure. The Mo-doped LDHs $@$ MOF-Se showed a high capacity of $5.16\mathsf{C}\thinspace\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ at $2\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , and maintained roughly $92\%$ capability at 10 mA $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , and $81.4\%$ of original capacity over 3000 cycles. The ASC device demonstrated an energy density of 41.3 Wh ${\bf k g}^{-1}$ , a power den­ sity of $750.0~\mathrm{W~kg}^{-1}$ , and a notably long lifecycle of $94\%$ of original capacitance after 10,000 charge/discharge cycles.
Tang et al. [112] were the first group to utilize CC to grow 1D cobalt carbonate hydroxide (CCH) and then derive LDH from MOF as a supercapacitor. The CCH nanorods were synthesized via a modest hy­ drothermal process using cobalt nitrate and urea to obtain $\mathsf{C C@C C H}$ Then, they grow ZIF-67 on $\mathsf{C C@C C H}$ as a sacrificing template. After the etching and ion exchanging processes in ethanol solution with Ni nitrate, NiCo-LDH was formed on the ZIF-67’s surface, and finally, $\mathbf{CC@CCH}.$ MOF-LDH was obtained (Fig. 6a). As can be seen in Fig. 6b-c, these 3D hybrid arrays (HAs) have several advantages regarding their unique structure, such as more synergistic effects because of the bimetallic arrangement, more active sites, shorter routes for ion transportation, and lack of aggregation, leading to better electrochemical performance. The CC@CCH-MOF-LDH HAs showed a high specific capacitance of $1700\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ at a current density of $2\mathrm{A}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , an excellent rate capacity of $1310\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $50\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ , the $78.2\%$ of capacitance retention, and a good lifespan of $91.1\%$ at $10\mathrm{A}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ after 2500 cycles.
As a self-sacrificing template, Yang et al. [113] employed leaf-like ZIF-67 MOFs that were synthesized directly on flexible CC. A thermal treatment in ethanol with Ni salt followed by ion exchange and etching reactions produced NiCo-LDH. Then, TAA as a sulfur source was used for direct sulfurization of CC@NiCo-LDH, resulting in the formation of selectively embedded cobalt sulfide, $\mathsf{C o}_{9}\mathsf{S}_{8}$ , NPs (CC@NiCo-LDH/ $\mathrm{Co}_{9}{\cal S}_{8})$ . The 2D structure of LDH boosts the interlayer ion transfer and diminishes the transportation resistance in the electrolyte. Besides, $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\ensuremath{S_{8}}$ NPs possess numerous redox-active sites that effectively improve structural stability. The interactions among NiCo-LDH, embedded $\mathrm{Co}_{9}{\ensuremath{S_{8}}}$ NPs, and the conductive substrate affect the electrochemical properties, conductivity, capacity, and stability, resulting in a high capacitance of $2438\mathrm{F}\ g^{-1}$ at 5 A ${\bf g}^{-1}$ of $\mathrm{CC}@\mathrm{NiCo-LDH/Co}98\$ electrode. Furthermore, the ASC of $\mathrm{CC}@\mathrm{NiCo-LDH/Co}98\$ with activated carbon reached the ultimate energy density of $38.0\mathrm{Wh}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ at $800.0\mathrm{W}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ and a superior life cycle with $93.1\%$ retention of the original capacitance after 5000 cycles.
In another work by Yang and co-workers [114], they used the quantum dots (QD) form of $\mathsf{C o}_{9}\mathsf{S}_{8}$ to produce a high-performance hybrid supercapacitor. They employed CF as the skeleton to develop 2D Zn-ZIF MOFs on it as a sacrificial agent. In the next step, in an autoclave, by adding Ni and Co chloride salt via coprecipitation and an ion etching process, they synthesized the ternary metal LDH, CF@NiCoZn-LDH. By modifying the ratio of Ni/Co metal ions, the distinctive structure of $\mathrm{CF}@\mathrm{Ni_{x}C o_{3-x}Z n-}$ LDH was successfully created and $\mathrm{CF}@\mathrm{Ni_{2}C o_{1}Z n}.$ LDH was the ideal compound. Then, they used TAA again for the in situ se­ lective sulfurization process to form embedded $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\ensuremath{S_{8}}$ -QDs within the interlayers (Fig. 6d). Inducing chemical species inside the layers is one of the current challenges. Also, the smaller scale of materials is more profitable; here, QD cobalt sulfides cause unique properties and expose more active sites in the structure. Additionally, 1D CF and 3D CFC are responsible for the noteworthy structural stability and improved mechanical flexibility. The as-obtained CF@NiCoZn-LDH/Co9S8-QD electrode delivered an electrochemical capacity of $350.6\ \mathrm{\mAh}\ \mathrm{\g}^{-1}$ $(2504\mathrm{~F~g~}^{-1})$ at $1\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}$ . Also, the ASC system with this electrode and carbon nanosheets improved with single-walled carbon nanotubes (CNS-SCN) was assembled and demonstrated an excellent energy den­ sity of 56.4 Wh ${\bf k g}^{-1}$ at a power density of $875~\mathrm{W~kg^{-1}}$ , with an outstanding capacity retention of $95.3\%$ after 8000 cycles. These supe­ rior electrochemical results are accomplished by the cutting-edge hier­ archical structure of $\mathrm{CF}@\mathrm{NiCoZn-LDH/Co_{9}}\mathrm{S_{8}}\mathrm{-QD}$ due to its exceptional properties such as rich active sites, structural solidity, mechanical flex­ ibility, and fast ion/electron transport, and the synergistic effects be­ tween LDHs and QDs.
In the same year, 2020, Xue et al. [115] directly produced a typical 3D LDH via a simple and facile procedure on CFC to achieve a highperformance supercapacitor in flexible devices. Similarly, as shown in Fig. 6e, they first synthesized 2D ZIF-67 arrays directly on the CFC substrate through in-situ hydrolysis of Co-MOF. Then by adding nickel nitrate salt and via a hydrothermal reaction, 3D NiCo-LDH was obtained due to the MOF conversion. This research group has previously syn­ thesized a similar structure with an indirect method, but this present work showed a lack of material agglomeration, fast ion transport, more active sites, and improved conductivity because of the conductive sub­ strate. Therefore, the NiCo-LDH/CFC exhibited an excellent specific capacitance of $2685\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ $(1074\mathrm{C}\ g^{-1})$ . Also, an assembled ACS device of active material and active carbon delivered a high energy density of 64.9 Wh ${\bf k g}^{-1}$ , a maximum power density of $16.9\mathrm{~kW~kg}^{-1}$ , and $82.7\%$ capacitance retention after 3000 cycles in the durability test.
Although Liu et al. used the direct growth technique to develop LDH structures on CC, they changed two variations; first, they used manga­ nese salt for further MOF to LDH transformation, and second, they focused on the anionic effects [116]. In the synthesis process, leaf-like ZIF-67 was selected as a template precursor to grow on CC. Then, the MOF derived to CoMn-LDH through an easy transformation in deionized (DI) water on stirrer. As a result of the instability of manganese(III) ions, the final LDH is notably unstable and changes nearly immediately in an alkaline solution. So here, they used three different Mn salts, $\mathrm{Mn}S\mathrm{O}_{4}$ , Mn $(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , and $\mathrm{MnCl}_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ , to investigate the best active phase to design effective electrode materials (Fig. 6f). Altering the anions had obvious effects on the layered structure, phase conversion, and charge storage properties. Among the three obtained CoMn-LDHs, the one with $\mathrm{MnSO}_{4}$ salt showed the largest interlayer spacing of $1.08~\mathrm{{nm}}$ and the maximum surface area, while the lattice spacing of manganese chloride and nitrate solutions was measured at 0.78 and $0.76~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , respectively. The structural stability results in the KOH environment revealed that CoMn-LDH- ${\cdot}s0_{4}$ maintained its layered structure well, but CoMn-LDH-Cl and CoMn-LDH- $\mathrm{NO}_{3}$ , through structural degradation, turned into spinel structures with a smaller surface area, resulting in underprivileged electrochemical performance. By comparing all the anions, the larger size of the $s0_{4}^{2-}$ ions results in morphological differences, more extended interlayer gallery space, and better electrochemical results. Therefore, the unique features of CoMn-LDH- $s0_{4}@\mathsf{C C}$ preserved its layered shape and structural stability, showed an areal capacity of $582.07\mathrm{mC}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ at $2\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , and its ASC cell demonstrated an energy density of $0.096\mathrm{\mWh}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ at a power density of $1.5\mathrm{\mW\cm^{-2}}$ .
In 2020, Liu et al. [117] used inexpensive Melamine foam (MF) as a pristine conductive substrate to load active materials after a multistep modification and synthesis process due to its unique properties such as low density, high porosity, 3D organized structure, and outstanding mechanical properties. Nevertheless, the extremely low conductivity of MF diminishes its applications. Converting MF into the conductive Ndoped porous carbon foam (NCF) through an annealing procedure at a high temperature is considered a practical approach to improve elec­ trolyte and ion transfer with minimum resistance. After annealing MF at $400^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under air atmosphere, the hollow primary N doped carbon foam was obtained. Subsequently, via a treatment in an ethanol flame, hollow conductive NCF was formed. Then, through an in-situ formation, ZIF-67 was synthesized and then via ion-exchange process converted into NiCoLDH on NCF. The gist of the fabrication process is shown in Fig. 6g. The 2D NiCo-LDH materials were embedded within the conductive frames of NCF, preventing the aggregation of LDH and increasing the charge transfer and redox reaction. Also, long-term cycling performance improved due to more exposed surface area and shortened channels of the 3D skeleton of NCF. The NiCo-LDH/NCF electrode exhibited a high specific capacity of $756\mathsf{C}\ \mathsf{g}^{-1}$ at a current density of $0.5\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}$ and maintained its specific capacity of $414\mathrm{C}\ g^{-1}$ at a current density of 20 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ . Likewise, the assembled ASC of NiCo-LDH@NCF with AC reached a high energy density of $41.5~\mathrm{Wh~kg^{-1}}$ at a power density of $750~\mathrm{Wkg^{-1}}$ and an outstanding stability of $80.4\%$ retention over 10,000 charge/ discharge cycles.
The large-lateral-size graphene with excellent stability and outstanding electrical conductivity is a desirable conductive carbon material as a supporting substrate. In addition, reduced graphene oxide (rGO) is a variant of graphene with numerous oxygen-containing groups, increasing the surface wettability, faradaic reaction sites, and electrochemical activity [118–120].
Bai et al. [121] utilized graphene directly as a current collector and a substrate for the adsorption of Co ions and a further precipitation pro­ cess for direct growth of ZIF-67. Then, through a thermal ion-exchange reaction with additional cobalt salt and extra oxidation treatment, hol­ low nanocages of Co-Co-LDH were homogenously placed on graphene nanosheets. Electrochemical examinations confirmed that $15~\mathrm{mg}$ of graphene in the Co–Co LDH/graphene composite showed greater per­ formance, delivering a high specific capacitance of $1205\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , excellent $60.3\%$ capacitance retention, and cycling stability with only $3.5\%$ capacitance loss after 2000 cycles. Also, the assembled ASC system presented a high energy density of $49.5\mathrm{Wh}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ and a maximum power density of $7000\mathrm{W}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ .
Yang et al. tailored a unique structure to solve the low intrinsic conductivity and agglomeration of LDH by using NF as the substrate and subsequential reduction of deposited graphene to reach rGO [122]. In this technique, 2D Co-ZIF was directly synthesized on rGO nanosheets on NF at room temperature, followed by an etching deposition proced­ ure using nickel nitrate, achieving “nanosheet-on-nanosheet” NiCo $\mathrm{LDH}@\mathrm{rGO/NF}$ (Fig. 7a). As mentioned before, the oxygen groups on the rGO expose more redox active sites, more conductivity, and faster charge transfer, boosting the electrochemical activity of the $\mathrm{LDH/rGO}$ hybrid. Therefore, the NiCo $\mathrm{LDH}@\mathrm{rGO/NF}$ electrode revealed ultrahigh specific capacitance of $2408.8\mathrm{F}\ g^{-1}$ at $0.5\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ , as well as its fabricated ASC with a high energy density of $32.2\mathrm{W}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ and excellent durability of $80.4\%$ retention throughout 10,000 cycles, demonstrating a prom­ ising material in actual applications.
Despite the previous substrates that have been described, NF is still preferable according to its advantageous properties. In the most recent paper published by Li et al., 2D Ni-ZIF MOFs were directly grown on NF as a conductive substrate [123]. Then, it was plunged into a nitrogendoped graphene quantum dots (N-GQD) solution, and N-GQDs were placed on the surface of the Ni-MOF. Through a hydrothermal process and adding gallium salt into the media, NiGa-LDH is derived from MOF.
The nanosheet structure, high conductive substrate, N-doped GQD, large surface area, and synergistic interaction between metal elements are noticeable factors for outstanding electrochemical performance. The NiGa-LDH/N-GQD/NF electrode exhibited a high specific capacitance of $2160\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at 1 A ${\bf g}^{-1}$ and a good lifecycle with a capacity retention rate of $87.5\%$ in 5000 cycles. Moreover, they also assembled an ASC device using NiGa-LDH/N-GQD/NF as the active material for the positive electrode and synthesized carbon NSs as the negative electrode. First, via coprecipitation, they developed nanosheet Co-MOF nanosheets on CC, and then, by calcination under the Ar atmosphere, carbon $\tt N S s/C C$ were conducted. The fabrication process of both electrodes can be seen from Fig. 7b. This device showed an energy density of 78.8 Wh ${\bf k g}^{-1}$ at a power density of 1432.7 W ${\bf k g}^{-1}$ , $81.2\%$ capacitance retention after 8000 cycles, and powered multiple LEDs in a series setup.
Zhou and colleagues [124] used a facile, energy-saving, and green synthesis method in water solution and at room temperature to form hollow NiCo-LDH nanoflakes via a two-step synthesis procedure. Fig. 7c shows that Co-MOF (ZIF-67) was first grown directly on NF, and after the hydrolysis of nickel sulfate and an etching reaction, it transformed into ultrathin nanoflakes of NiCo-LDH. Using nanosheet Co-MOFs on NF and the nanoflake structure of LDH provide a short ion diffusion pathway and abundant active sites. The NiCo-LDH/NF showed a great specific capacity of $2148\mathrm{F}\ g^{-1}$ at a current density of $1\mathrm{A}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and good cycling stability of $82\%$ capacity retention after 1000 cycles.
An innovative method to achieve a high-performance supercapacitor was reported by Zhu et al. [125]. They applied a three-step synthesis strategy, which includes chemical deposition, calcination, and electro­ deposition. First, bimetallic ZnCo-MOFs were directly deposited on NF as a sacrificial template. Secondly, by using a simple calcination process, the zinc‑cobalt oxide (ZnCoO) network was derived from the MOF and sustained the original morphology of the MOF. The structure of the transition bimetallic oxides became porous with a large specific surface area and more redox-active sites due to its mixed valence structure. Also, it elevated the electron diffusion rate by shortening the charge transfer routes. Finally, through the electrodeposition process, ultrathin NiCoLDH nanosheets were grown on the surface of ZnCoO NS to develop a core/shell nanostructure that can further expand the number of elec­ troactive sites. The synergy between transition bimetallic oxides and hydroxide as well as the core/shell structure improved electrode mate­ rials’ stability and electrochemical properties. The ZnCoO/NiCo-LDH/ NF exhibited outstanding specific capacitance of $2275.2\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ at 1 A ${\bf g}^{-1}$ and a notable life cycle of $82.4\%$ capacitance retention after 5000 cycles. Moreover, the ASC device reached an energy density of 44.5 Wh $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ at a power density of $800\mathrm{W}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ with acceptable stability of $71.5\%$ retention after 5000 cycles.
A high-performance heterostructure material was designed and produced by Acharya et al. to be used as a positive electrode in a supercapacitor [126]. This structure is composed of two parts: the first part is a core that is built from 1D zinc‑nickel‑cobalt (ZNC) nanoneedles that were directly grown on NF by a hydrothermal method in an alkaline solution and then calcined (ZNCO); the second part is a shell is made of 2D CoNi-LDH nanosheets that were obtained from ZIF-67 MOF on nanoneedles through co-precipitation and ion-exchange (Fig. 6d). The direct transformation of the LDH in the system prevents selfagglomeration and results in better structural stability and perfor­ mance. The well interconnected 1D core and 2D shell built a 3D porous ZNCO $@$ CoNi-LDH with a large specific surface area, rich electroactive sites, fast charge/mass transfer, and enhanced electrical conductivity. The $\mathsf{Z N C O@C o}$ -Ni-LDH electrode presented a high specific capacitance of $2866\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $1\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ with capacitance retention of $68.35\%$ at a higher current density of $10\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}$ and outstanding durability of $89\%$ capaci­ tance retention after 8000 cycles. Additionally, its ASC device with the obtained electrode as positive and N-doped graphene hydrogel (NGH) as a negative electrode demonstrated a high specific capacitance of $178\mathrm{F}$ ${\bf g}^{-1}$ at 1 A g-1, great rate capability of $70.22\%$ at $10\mathrm{~A~g{-}1}$ , superior lifetime of $91.2\%$ after 8000 cycles at $10\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ , and a maximum energy density of $63.28~\mathrm{Wh}~\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ at a power density of $796.53\mathrm{W}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$
## 3. Batteries: Direct and indirect fabrication
A supercapattery is a state-of-the-art category of electrochemical energy storage device that combines from a battery-type (high energy density) and a supercapacitor-type (high power density and long cycle life) electrodes. It also has certain other advantageous characteristics, such as the fast charge ability, maintain stability over lengthy cycles, and operate at adequate paces. The electrode materials are crucial in maximizing the supercapattery [127,128]. For the purpose of maxi­ mizing the device performance of supercapattery in connection with energy capacity, power capability, cyclic stability, and cost efficiency, numerous studies have lately been conducted to effectively design and produce innovative supercapattery-type electrode materials with particular physical and chemical characteristics [129]. For instance, a template-directed strategy was used by Zhou et al. [130] for in situ syntheses of a novel combination of crystalline and amorphous phases as a supercapattery material to include an amorphous phase into the crystalline MDL materials, accomplishing improved electrodes that can be seen in Fig. 8a. They directly synthesized ZIF-67 on nickel foam in this strategy and then transformed it into NiCo-LDH nanosheets. Employing an inexpensive, green, and oxidative polarization method in $0.1\mathrm{MK_{2}B_{4}O_{7}{\cdot}4H_{2}O\left(K{-}B_{i}\right)}$ electrolyte, the surface amorphization of the latest product (NF/NiCo-LDH) occurred and converted into NiCo-borate (Ni-Co-LDH/Ni-Co-Bi). High porosity, elevated conductivity, and rela­ tively high activity are only a few of the improved physicochemical qualities of hybrid material, thanks to the inclusion of amorphous NiCoBi. Additionally, the negatively charged borate groups and the wellchanged electronic structure simplify the low entropy energy during the electrochemical reaction procedures, and it would be more advanta­ geous for the inherent deficiencies of LDHs. As a result, the composite, which is consisted of the crystalline and amorphous phases, exhibits a high specific capacity of 891C ${\bf g}^{-1}$ at $\boldsymbol{1}\mathrm{~A~}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ and a maximal energy density of 62.8 Wh $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ at a power density of $800~\mathrm{W~kg^{-1}}$ , and good capacity stability of $81\%$ after 5000 cycles.
MDLs have recently turned into the most promising direct and in­ direct cathode materials in batteries due to their high surface area and porosity, as well as their noticeable electrical conductivity. Lith­ ium‑sulfur batteries (LSBs) are a developing energy storage system due to their low synthesis cost, high theoretical capacity and energy density, high storage ability, and safety [131,132]. The sulfur to $\mathrm{Li}_{2}S$ conversion reaction in LSBs is where the enormous capacity originates. The in­ sulators, S and $\mathrm{Li}_{2}S$ , necessitate the addition of conductive additives that cause weak operation of the active material. However, the highly soluble redox intermediate lithium polysulfides $\left(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathbf{S}_{\mathbf{x}}\right)$ can easily migrate through the electrolyte, cathode, and anode, leading to significant ca­ pacity loss and further application limitations. As a research subject of LSBs, synthesizing advanced materials to stabilize dissolved poly­ sulfides, minimize low Coulombic efficiency and self-discharge behav­ iors, control volume alteration during cycles, and elongate lifecycles is crucial [133,134]. For example, Xu et al. [135] successfully prepared an effective LSB cathode coated on an aluminum sheet by transforming ZIF67 into NiCo-LDH covered with rGO and then further sulfurization in $\mathsf{C S}_{2}$ solution to form a hollow structure of S/LDH/rGO (Fig. 8b). In this composition represented in Fig. 8c, the LDH is responsible for the adsorption of lithium polysulfides, the covered LDH by rGO for high specific surface area, and the rGO itself for the high-rate electronic conductivity, boosting the electrochemical performance of $S/\mathrm{LDH/rGO}$ and speeding the electron transmissions. At current densities of 1C and 0.2C, the discharge capacity of $S/\mathrm{LDH/rGO}$ was calculated at $730~\mathrm{{mAh}}$ ${\bf g}^{-1}$ and $958~\mathrm{mAh~g^{-1}}$ , respectively, with $99\%$ of coulombic efficiency.
Lithium-ion batteries (LIBs) are emerging as viable energy storage devices due to their rapid rate of charging and discharging, high energy density, great cyclability, and the dominant power source for day-to-day portable electronic devices. Using graphite as a common anode material according to its low theoretical specific capacity is insufficient to fulfill the rising demand for energy storage. As a result, it has been problematic for LIBs to reach higher energy densities. Searching for innovative, alternative anode materials with novel compositions, distinctive struc­ tures and high theoretical and practical specific capacities is indeed required [132,136,137]. As a matter of fact, Duan and colleagues [138] fabricated a 3D double transition metal phosphide (TMP) consisting of Ni and Co (NiCoP) from further phosphorization treatment of NiCo-LDH nanocage with $\mathrm{{NaH}_{2}\mathrm{{PO}_{2}}}$ , which itself was derived from ZIF-67 as shown in Fig. 8d and its further morphology characterizations in Fig. 8(e-h). The outstanding electrochemical performance of NiCoP nanocages is linked to the inherent shape of the template, high surface area, the large number of micro-holes and channels that improve the electric contact and ion transport, as well as the existence of phosphide groups in the nanocage structure after the phosphidation process. Finally, the wellprepared NiCoP showed excellent performance in LIBs as an anode, with a reversible capacity of $622\mathrm{~mAh~g^{-1}}$ at $100\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , an ultrahigh capacity of $373\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ , and good capacity retention of $104\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ after 100 cycles both at $1000\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . Additionally, in 2022, Kim et al. [139] stated the synthesis of new advanced material as LIB’s anode through additional modification of MDL. These multicomponent nano­ fibers were directly synthesized on nickel foam with a conductive matrix consisting of porous N-doped reduced graphene oxide (N-doped rGO). ZIF-67 MOFs were grafted onto an rGO substrate and transformed into NiCo-LDH. Further treatments were conducted to convert the LDH, as mentioned earlier, into a layered double oxide (NiCo-LDO). Their advanced and innovative synthesis procedures are shown thoroughly in Fig. 8i and j. The porous, conductive, and well-designed anode, P-(Ni, $\mathbf{Co})0/\mathbf{rGO}$ NFs, exhibited high structural stability, numerous reaction sites, and quick Li-ion diffusion routes with conductive channels, lead­ ing to lengthy cycle stability of $907\ \mathrm{mA}\mathrm{~h~g^{-1}~}$ after 500 cycles and a sufficient capability of $519\mathrm{mA}$ h ${\bf g}^{-1}$ at $5.0\mathrm{A}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ .
Wang et al. [140] directly developed co-based MOFs on CFC and then optimized them to $\mathrm{Co(OH)}_{2}@\mathrm{NiCo-}$ LDH nanowire arrays to attain higher electrical capacity for further usage in alkaline zinc ion batteries (AZIBs). Recently, these types of batteries have attracted a great deal of research interest due to the various benefits of metallic zinc $(\mathrm{Zn})$ , such as the high redox potential of zinc against the standard hydrogen electrode that enables the battery to function in aqueous solutions; a considerable theoretical capacity of $820\mathrm{\mAh}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ ; and an affordable price and abundant availability. Anodes, aqueous electrolytes, separators, and cathodes form AZIBs. Zinc metal has been the most frequent anode for AZIBs. However, the cathodes are compounds that permit the reversible insertion and extraction of $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ . Currently, zinc is typically overloaded with bulk, resulting in the underuse of zinc metal and a relatively poor energy density, which limits the practicability of AZIBs. As a result, achieving a well-designed and high-performance cathode is a robust approach for AZIBs [141,142]. Therefore, they claimed that the final product was a stable core-shell nanostructure with a Setaria viridis shape that possessed a high surface area and abundant redox-active sites, providing benefits for energy storage. The as-prepared A-Co $(\mathrm{OH})_{2}@$ NiCo-LDH revealed a high specific capacity of $285.9\:\mathrm{mAh}\:\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $0.5\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ and an outstanding capacity retention of $72.9\%$ between 0.5 A ${\bf g}^{-1}$ and $50\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ . In addition, they fabricated a hybrid supercapacitor (HSC) and an alkaline $Z\mathrm{n}$ -ion battery (AZIB) with $\mathsf{A-C o(O H)_{2}@N i C o-}$ LDH as the cathode. These devices exhibited exceptional energy den­ sities of $40.6\mathrm{Wh}\mathrm{kg1}$ at a power density of $1.78\mathrm{kW}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ and 489.6 Wh ${\bf k g}^{-1}$ at a power density of $0.38\mathrm{~kW~kg}^{-1}$ , and a wonderfully long cycle life of GCD cycles.
Aqueous rechargeable batteries (ARBs) have recently attracted renewed academic study, primarily because of the appealing qualities of water-based electrolytes. Additionally, a high rate of battery operation may be achieved by its naturally high ionic conductivity (in comparison with other organic solvents). In addition, water is readily available, easy to control, and a tremendously soluble solvent. As a result, various affordable electrolyte solutions may be created for specific battery chemistries using water as a flexible platform. Numerous studies on ARBs have been motivated by the appealing characteristics of aqueous electrolytes [141,143,144]. In 2018, Zhang et al. [145] first reported a unique approach to synthesizing a twisted structure as a cathode in ARBs and wearable energy-storage devices. They formed an entangled elec­ trode through a two-part fabrication process, using carbon nanotube fiber (CNTF) as a current collector and a conductive substrate, Fig. 8k. In the first part, they directly developed ZnCo-ZIF on CNTF. While adding zinc into the solution, it was converted into a ternary LDH termed NiZnCo-LDH/ZnCo-MOF. After that, the final material was obtained via a complete phosphorization process, NiZnCoP@CNTF. In the second part, the CNTF was activated by an oxidation process, and then ferric chloride and terephthalic acid were added to the solution to form FeMOF on oxidized CNTF (OCNTF). Afterward, through an additional annealing process at $350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ , $\mathsf{S}{-}\alpha{-}\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{OCNTF}$ was achieved. Subse­ quently, two final products were used as cathode and anode, respec­ tively, in a KOH-PVA gel electrolyte and underwent further annealing and solidifying to fabricate a twisted fiber-shaped electrode material for aqueous rechargeable batteries (FARBs): $\mathrm{S{-}\alpha{-}F e_{2}O_{3}/O C N T F//N i Z n-}$ $\mathsf{C o P@C N T F}$ . This fantastic fabrication method and directly developing the materials on the current collector enhanced the electrical and me­ chanical features, tackling the powder form electrode problems. The assembled electrode produced an incredible capacity of $0.092\mathrm{\mAh}$ $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and an energy density of $30.61\mathrm{mWh}\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ with only insignificant capacity deterioration after 3000 cycles.
## 4. MOF-derived LDHs as bifunctional electrode materials
In addition to all the applications mentioned earlier, MDLs are also being developed in other fields of study. One of the most intriguing ideas to pursue is the development of bifunctional materials that can be used for a wide variety of purposes. Recently, rechargeable batteries have attracted significant interest as one of the most promising energy con­ version systems in the near future. In 2019, Qian et al. [146] introduced the first bifunctional rechargeable Zn-air batteries (RZABs) with both OER and ORR active sites by using MDL materials. In this Janus system, as demonstrated in Fig. $9\mathrm{a}$ , they prepared a conductive MOF-derived carbon network (MCN) by pyrolyzing ZIF-8/NaCl composite and then attached NiFe-LDH nanoplates on MCNs via a hydrothermal process as ORR- and OER-active sites, respectively. The in-situ growth of LDH on the conductive and porous support is one of the critical factors for enhanced durability of both half-cells and extended working hours. As evidence, the MCN-LDH endured over $100\mathrm{h}$ in RZAB assemblies under a current density of $10\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ without exhibiting any noticeable per­ formance damage and revealed a triple life period when compared to a well-known noble metal catalyst, $\mathrm{Pt/C{\cdot}R u O_{2}}$ with a constant charge and discharge process for a minimum of 5 days at a current density of $10\mathrm{mA}$ $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . In 2020, Ding et al. [147] continued their investigations on rechargeable batteries regarding lithium‑oxygen batteries (LOBs). In comparison with traditional Li–ion batteries, Li–air or $\mathrm{Li}{-}0_{2}$ batteries are practical alternatives. These batteries require additional modifica­ tions due to their larger overpotential, lower cycling performance, and reciprocating efficiency arising from unavoidable side reactions; low reaction kinetics for ORR and OER; decomposition of the electrolyte; and slow transport rates of the reactants and electrons. Additionally, the existence of insoluble and insulated $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ is another obstacle in LOBs. However, it has been found that the exceptional properties of MOFs and their derivatives would lead them to satisfactory materials in this field [132,148,149]. This two-step reaction produced porous NiCo-LDH from ZIF-67 via hydrolysis to release ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ into the environment and form the ultimate LDH. Then they employed a hydrothermal process to grow $\mathrm{{MnO}}_{2}$ nanosheets on template-directed LDHs evenly (Fig. 9b). The twodimensional ${\delta\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{{MnO}}_{2}$ operates as an outstanding ORR catalyst, benefiting from strong catalytic activity, eco-friendliness, and cost-efficiency. Fig. 9 (c-f) shows the morphology and combination of hollow and lamellar structures in $\mathrm{NiCo-LDH/MnO_{2}}$ that leads to a large surface area and numerous available active sites, resulting in a rapid diffusion pathway for released ${\bf L i}^{+}$ and different oxygen species. Also, it can moderate the $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ growth on the surface of the electrode during the ORR, causing better charge transfer and battery capacity. The $\mathrm{NiCo-LDH/MnO_{2}}$ cathode demonstrated superior charge/discharge specific capacity of $13,380\ \mathrm{mA}\mathrm{h}\ \mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $100\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , low overpotential of $0.63\mathrm{~V~}$ , and un­ interrupted cycling durability for 162 cycles at a settled capacity of 500 mAh $\displaystyle{\boldsymbol{\mathbf{g}}}^{-1}$ . However, many challenges associated with LOBs should be tackled to prepare them for more practical applications. Wang and coworkers [150] reported a bifunctional double hydroxide as a supercapacitor and a battery. In this novel strategy, they formed NiCodouble hydroxide from MOF arrays on nickel foam as conductive sub­ strates. The as-obtained materials efficiently utilized for a hybrid supercapacitor and a $\mathtt{N i-Z n}$ aqueous secondary battery, resulting in a remarkable reversible specific capacitance of $325.6\ \mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $2\ \mathrm{mA}$ $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and rate performance of $75.5\%$ of primary capacity retention at 40 mA $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . Additionally, as a NiCo-Zn battery, the mentioned electrode displayed a strong specific capacity of $329\mathrm{\mAh\}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and acceptable stability in various cycles. Likewise, the collected asymmetric super­ capacitor of NiCo-DH//AC disclosed an extraordinary capacity of 0.359 $\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ at $0.5\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-1}}$ along with exceptional capacitance retention of $91\%$ after 5000 cycles. Another pioneering study to synthesize com­ plex bifunctional materials have been conducted by Wang et al. [151], where the as-synthesized composite can be employed as a promising candidate in both the catalysis and energy storage fields. They obtained an innovative “shell-in-shell” nanostructure, assembled by etching the pristine NPs and growing the LDHs on their surfaces simultaneously. They also claimed that these two processes are noticeably competitive and can change the result of the conversions. The ratios of the water in the solvent and the metal nitrite (in the growing process) and the morphology of MOFs were mentioned as critical aspects to regulating the etching and growth. In a general manner, they first synthesized cubic ZIF-67, and then, via a seed-growth procedure, the cubic and rhombic dodecahedron (RD) morphologies of ZIF-8 were formed on ZIF-67, and the final structures of ZIF-67 $@$ ZIF-8 NPs were obtained. At the same time, the surfaces of the two MOFs were progressively etched and con­ verted into hollow shell-in-shell nanocages of LDH. The cubic and RD morphologies were selected due to their vivid shapes and sizes, which can ease the identification and comparison stages. In addition, they studied critical factors to achieve the optimized product. It was found that: 1) by increasing the ratio of water in the ethanol/water solvent, the hydrolysis of metal ions to form both cubic and RD morphologies ac­ celerates, leading to a higher etch rate that causes the structure collapse of ZIF-8 instead of preserving its shape as an LDH template. 2) The concentration of metal nitrate influences the conversion process; the higher the concentration of $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ in the solution, the faster ZIF-8 etches, resulting shorter time for LDH shells to grow evenly. 3) To further exemplify the unique results of using $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ in the conversion process, the metal nitrate salt was changed to $\mathrm{Fe}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{3}$ , indicating the same results but over a more extended period due to $\mathrm{Fe}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{3}$ ’s slower rate of hydrolysis.
Aside from the aforementioned electrochemical applications, the MDL materials have demonstrated remarkable capabilities in a variety of other fields as well. For instance, some of the ground-breaking ap­ plications of MDLs are in photocatalytic hydrogen evolution reaction (ZnIn2S4@NiFe-LDH) [152], water scarcity (CoFe-LDH) [153], epoxy nanocomposite production with lower fire hazard (rGO@NiCo-LDH) [154], the release of Ciprofloxacin in drug-delivery (MgAl-LDH@por­ ousFe3O4) [155], purification and separation of proteins (Fe3O4 $@$ NiFeLDH) [156], and last but not least, non-invasive biosensors to detect glucose (NiCo-LDH NS@GNR and annealed CC/CuCo-LDH) [157,158] and lactate in human sweat (NiCo-LDH) [159].
Another noteworthy issue is that, as mentioned in this review article, surprisingly, most of the MDL research was based on the cobalt MOF. Indeed, the distinctive physiochemical properties of cobalt as a metal element or in MOF structure are not hidden from anyone; however, it is worth the effort to work on other metal-based MOFs, such as aluminum, as future sacrificial templates to reduce the use of cobalt and use more abundant and less toxic precursors instead [29,160].
## 5. Conclusion and future perspective
In conclusion, with increasing energy demands, achieving highperformance materials such as electrocatalysts in water splitting and electrochemical electrodes in supercapacitors and batteries are of great importance in energy storage and conversion (ESC) systems. Over the years, MOFs have shown good capability in ESC as a precursor to modify with post-treatments or as a sacrificial template to derive other mate­ rials. MOF-derived LDHs (MDLs) are one of the most favorable and versatile MOF-derived materials due to their unique properties such as tunability, structural stability, inherent morphology from the MOF, and others. Numerous studies have been conducted to investigate the elec­ trochemical performance of these materials. This review summarized recent progress and developments in MDLs by providing innovative synthesis techniques and outstanding electrochemical experiment re­ sults. We also categorized MDLs into applications (supercapacitor, water splitting, and battery) and synthesis methods (direct or indirect syn­ thesis on a substrate). As a result, synthesizing MLDs on a conductive substrate can boost the electrochemical activities, as well as other postsynthesis treatments such as phosphorization, sulfurization, seleniza­ tion, metal doping, annealing, and others. As the unique properties of MDLs were previously highlighted, the production of these materials represents a novel approach to materials derived from MOFs. Using these materials and their various synthesis techniques can compensate for the structural and conductive defects of MOFs, allowing them to be employed with better and higher efficiency. MDLs can possess the fea­ tures of LDHs in addition to those of MOFs such as unique structure, high tunability, redox active sites, and electroconductivity, making them superior to most other materials derived from MOFs. The main goal in this research field is to design new advanced materials with our chem­ ical knowledge and then synthesize functional materials to achieve highperformance electrocatalysts and electrode materials. MDLs are among the new developing structures that can be modified to achieve optimal electrode materials in different fields, such as supercapacitors, batteries, and water-splitting reactions. However, we believe that there are several issues that should be tackled and worked on in future research, as described below:
1) The low conductivity of MLD materials.   
2) Relatively expensive materials and synthesis methods.   
3) Using other potential self-sacrificial templates instead of mostly Cobased MOFs.   
4) Designing and achieving more bi-or multi-functional active materials.   
5) Developing more rechargeable electrode materials in batteries
These challenges are the most significant ones that still a consider­ able amount of research needs to be done in order to address them successfully. A variety of strategies should be explored to develop a higher conductivity material. Researchers can reduce costs by designing more intelligent synthesis methods and by attempting to use green methods and widely accessible materials, making the process not only cost-effective but also environmentally friendly. In addition, researchers are encouraged to produce more advanced structures that can serve multiple energy storage purposes. Additionally, in order to reduce en­ ergy consumption in the industry, it is necessary to look into the further development of rechargeable batteries. We have high hopes that in­ dividuals looking into new and different ideas for producing unique advanced materials would find our research helpful in identifying and overcoming the barriers they currently face and achieving improved performance due to their efforts.
